---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 20</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Eight questions &middot; 15 minutes &middot; half of them select-all
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Due session 62, the last homework before you submit.</b> Paper and
            pencil, no computer, and the content is all Quarter 1 and Quarter 2.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Four of these eight are select-all-that-apply, and that is the
            whole reason this set exists.</b> Eight of the exam's seventy
            questions are select-all, and they are the ones students lose most
            often &mdash; not because the content is harder, but because{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">a select-all is only right if you pick
            every correct option and no incorrect one.</b> Partial credit does
            not exist. Stopping at the first true statement scores zero.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Set a timer for 15 minutes.</b> On the select-all questions, read
            every option before choosing any of them, and decide true or false on
            each one separately. Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            Which of these are true of a procedure with parameters, in the exam
            reference language?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The arguments are assigned to the parameters in order</li>
            <li>(B) <code>RETURN(expression)</code> may appear at any point and causes an immediate return</li>
            <li>(C) Every procedure must return a value</li>
            <li>(D) A returned value can be captured with <code>result &larr; procName(arg)</code></li>
            <li>(E) A procedure's parameters can be used by statements outside the procedure</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>Which selection forms does the exam reference language provide?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>IF</code>, <code>ELSE IF</code>, and <code>ELSE</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>IF</code> on its own, and <code>IF</code> with <code>ELSE</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>IF</code>, <code>ELSE</code>, and <code>SWITCH</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>IF</code> with <code>ELSE</code> only</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            Which of these are true of the two iteration forms on the reference
            sheet?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> provides no loop counter variable</li>
            <li>(B) <code>REPEAT UNTIL(condition)</code> repeats until the condition becomes true</li>
            <li>(C) <code>REPEAT UNTIL(condition)</code> repeats for as long as the condition is true</li>
            <li>(D) <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> takes no parentheses</li>
            <li>(E) The sheet also provides a counted loop that supplies an index</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4</h3>

          <p>Which values can <code>RANDOM(2, 5)</code> return?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) 2, 3, 4, or 5</li>
            <li>(B) 2, 3, or 4</li>
            <li>(C) 3, 4, or 5</li>
            <li>(D) Any number between 2 and 5, including fractions</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>Which of these statements about compression are accurate?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Lossless compression allows the original to be reconstructed completely</li>
            <li>(B) Lossy compression usually saves more space than lossless</li>
            <li>(C) Fewer bits always means less information</li>
            <li>(D) How much is saved depends on the redundancy in the original and on the algorithm used</li>
            <li>(E) Lossy compression is the right choice when quality matters most</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 6</h3>

          <p>In a binary number, the value of a position is:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) the base multiplied by the position, counting from 1 on the left</li>
            <li>(B) always a multiple of 8</li>
            <li>(C) the base raised to the power of the position, counting from 0 on the left</li>
            <li>(D) the base raised to the power of the position, counting from 0 on the right</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 7 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>Which of these statements are accurate?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Data must be in sorted order to use binary search</li>
            <li>(B) Linear search checks each element in order until the value is found or every element has been checked</li>
            <li>(C) An algorithm's efficiency can be informally measured by counting how many times a statement executes</li>
            <li>(D) Different correct algorithms for the same problem always have the same efficiency</li>
            <li>(E) Exponential and factorial efficiencies are examples of running in an unreasonable amount of time</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 8</h3>

          <p>An undecidable problem is one for which:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) an algorithm exists, but it runs too slowly to be practical</li>
            <li>(B) no instance of the problem has an algorithmic solution</li>
            <li>(C) no algorithm can be written that always gives a correct yes-or-no answer for every input</li>
            <li>(D) the answer depends on which programming language is used</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Eight answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far
            you got when the timer went off. Worth <b>4 points</b>, graded on
            completion.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And one written answer, in two or three sentences:</b> four of
            these were select-all. Say what you did differently on those four
            from what you did on the other four, and name the one you were least
            sure of.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
